WRITE(2)                       P32W Unix                        WRITE(2)

NAME
       write - write to a file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       ssize_t write(int fd, const void *buf, size_t n);

DESCRIPTION
       write writes n bytes from buf to the open file fd at the
       file offset (at the end of the file if it was opened with
       O_APPEND) and advances the offset by the number written. A
       regular file grows as needed; writing past its end leaves a
       hole that reads as zeros.

       Writes to a regular file go to the buffer cache; the kernel
       writes them to the disk within 30 seconds, or at sync(2).

       A write to a pipe waits while the pipe is full (it holds
       1024 bytes) until a reader makes room. If no one has the
       read end open, the writer gets the signal SIGPIPE (which
       kills it unless caught or ignored) and the call fails with
       EPIPE.

       A write to a terminal queues the bytes for output, waiting
       while the queue is full; with CRMOD set a newline goes out
       as carriage return, newline (see ioctl(2)).

       /dev/null discards everything. /dev/port writes each byte
       to the output port in turn (the last stays).

RETURN VALUE
       The number of bytes written, which is n unless the disk
       filled up or a signal interrupted a pipe or terminal write;
       -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EBADF   fd is not open, or not open for writing.
       EFAULT  buf is a bad address.
       EINVAL  n is larger than 2^31-1.
       EPIPE   A pipe with no reader (SIGPIPE is sent too).
       EINTR   A signal arrived before anything was written to a
               pipe or terminal.
       EFBIG   The file would grow past 2^31-1 bytes.
       ENOSPC  The filesystem has no free block.
       EIO     A disk error on a block device or /dev/rdisk.
       ENXIO   The device does not support writing (/dev/mem), or
               /dev/tty is used without a controlling terminal.
       ENODEV  The device has no write routine.

SEE ALSO
       read(2), open(2), lseek(2), pipe(2), sync(2), stdio(3)

BUGS
       Writes to a pipe are not atomic: when the pipe fills, the
       data of two writers can interleave, whatever the size. There
       is no PIPE_BUF.
